DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppbbmmrreedduuccee reads a PBM image as input and reduces it by a factor  of  _N,
       producing a PBM image as output.

       ppbbmmrreedduuccee  duplicates  a  lot  of the functionality of ppaammddiitthheerrbbww; you
       could do something like pamscale | pamditherbw, but ppbbmmrreedduuccee is a  lot
       faster.

       You  can use ppbbmmrreedduuccee to "re-halftone" an image.  Let's say you have a
       scanner that only produces black&white, not grayscale, and  it  does  a
       terrible  job  of  halftoning (most b&w scanners fit this description).
       One way to fix the halftoning is to scan at the highest possible  reso‐
       lution,  say  300 dpi, and then reduce by a factor of three or so using
       ppbbmmrreedduuccee.  You can even correct the brightness of an image,  by  using
       the --vvaalluuee option.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppbbmmrreedduuccee recognizes  the
       following command line options:




       --tthhrreesshhoolldd
              By  default,  ppbbmmrreedduuccee  does the halftoning after the reduction
              via boustrophedonic Floyd-Steinberg  error  diffusion;  however,
              you  can  use  this option to specify simple thresholding.  This
              gives better results when reducing line drawings.


       --ffllooyydd, --ffss
              Specify the Floyd-Steinberg error diffusion method.  This is the
              default.


       --vvaalluuee

              This option alters the thresholding value for all quantizations.
              It should be a real number between 0 and  1.   Above  0.5  means
              darker images; below 0.5 means lighter.


       --rraannddoommsseeeedd==_i_n_t_e_g_e_r
              This  is  the seed for the random number generator that controls
              the halftoning.

              Use this to ensure you get the same image  on  separate  invoca‐
              tions.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.75 (June 2016).





